# DevBridge 0.7.0 .test reference
#
# Run from the DevBridge.pak source directory with one or more connections:
#   python3 tools/devbridge.py --connect 192.168.0.144:brick run example.test
#   python3 tools/devbridge.py --adb SERIAL=brick run example.test
#
# This file is intentionally comments-only. Copy a recipe below, remove its
# leading `# ` markers, and adjust the device, ROM, and state slot.

# Session and connections
# SESSION <name> [output_root]       // {date}, {time}, and strftime fields work in name
# DISCOVER [timeout_seconds]         // listens for DevBridge LAN beacons
# CONNECT <ip_address> [alias]       // SSH/SCP connection
# CONNECT_ADB <serial> [alias]       // adb shell/adb pull connection
# ALIAS <old_alias> <new_alias>
# ALIAS_CMD <name> <command...>
# STATUS
#
# CLI connections occur before commands in this file. SSH and ADB devices may
# coexist. Prefix commands with `alias:` or `all:` when multiple devices are
# connected. An unprefixed device command requires exactly one connection.

# Inventory and terminal access
# GET_BTNS
# GET_PAKS
# GET_EMUS
# GET_ROM_LIST
# GET_ROM_BY_SYSTEM <tag>
# GET_ROM_SAVESTATES <tag> <rom>
# TRM <command...>                    // root-equivalent escape hatch
# KEEP_SCREEN_ON                      // reliable while DevBridge UI is foreground
#
# ROM inventory rows are: TAG<TAB>path-relative-to-Roms.
# Save-state rows are: SLOT<TAB>absolute_path<TAB>bytes<TAB>modified_epoch.
# Slot 9 is MinArch's hidden/automatic resume slot; `auto` is accepted when
# launching it.

# Controller input
# BTN <button>                        // 80 ms tap
# BTN HOLD <button-or-chord> <ms>
# BTN PRESS <button-or-chord>
# BTN RELEASE <button-or-chord>
# BTN REPEAT <button-or-chord> DURATION <ms> FREQUENCY <ms>
# BTN STOP                            // releases every held button
#
# Chords use commas, for example: BTN HOLD L1,R1 500
# Buttons: up down left right a b x y l1 r1 l2 r2 l3 r3 select start menu
# Repeats are always finite. With no options, REPEAT lasts 500 ms at 500 ms.

# Launch lifecycle
# OPEN_GAME <tag> <rom>
# OPEN_GAME_STATE <tag> <rom> <slot>
# OPEN_RANDOM_GAME <tag>
# OPEN_PAK <name-or-path>
# QUIT_GAME                           // MinArch standard five-row menu adapter
# WAIT_FOR <foreground> [timeout_ms]  // normally game, devbridge, or nextui
#
# OPEN_GAME_STATE verifies that the state belongs to the ROM and active core,
# then uses MinArch's normal /tmp/resume_slot.txt startup mechanism.

# Observation
# SCREEN [label]
# PERF [duration_ms] [interval_ms]
# WAIT <milliseconds>
#
# SCREEN captures the visible framebuffer page. PERF is functional telemetry,
# not a calibrated low-overhead benchmark: it performs remote /proc/sysfs
# polling. Do not use current PERF results for small performance comparisons.

# Commands planned but NOT implemented in 0.7.0 include PUSH, DEPLOY_PAK,
# ASSERT, transactional CONFIG commands, FINALLY, buffered PERF_START/STOP,
# true multi-device barriers, and READ_MENU. See PROTOCOL.md for the roadmap.

# Complete SSH lifecycle example
# SESSION tetris-{date}-{time} devbridge-sessions
# STATUS
# GET_ROM_BY_SYSTEM GB
# OPEN_GAME GB "Game Boy (GB)/Tetris (World) (Rev 1).zip"
# WAIT_FOR game 30000
# WAIT 1500
# SCREEN tetris-running
# PERF 3000 1000
# QUIT_GAME
# SCREEN devbridge-returned
# STATUS

# Save-state launch example
# SESSION state-{date}-{time} devbridge-sessions
# GET_ROM_SAVESTATES GBC "Game Boy Color (GBC)/Tetris DX (World) (SGB Enhanced) (GB Compatible).zip"
# OPEN_GAME_STATE GBC "Game Boy Color (GBC)/Tetris DX (World) (SGB Enhanced) (GB Compatible).zip" 9
# WAIT_FOR game 30000
# WAIT 1500
# SCREEN loaded-state-9
# QUIT_GAME
# SCREEN devbridge-returned

# Mixed transport example (normally connect these in the CLI or REPL)
# SESSION two-bricks-{date}-{time} devbridge-sessions
# CONNECT 192.168.0.144 wifi
# CONNECT_ADB BRICK_SERIAL usb
# all:STATUS
# wifi:GET_ROM_BY_SYSTEM GB
# usb:GET_ROM_BY_SYSTEM GBC
